      {
        "id": 122860,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-12-15T05:54:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"bluedarky\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/bluedarky/48/1975_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> bluedarky:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Sefirit wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;Pydro wrote:<br><blockquote>Originally, that line was in every fowl card, but it caused problems with some mechanics, like Hair Trigger Reflexes. For the new edition, thry took the line off the birds and put it on Matty. However, the over-sized card is from the old version. So, if 6ou used the new cards with the over-sized card, that text was lost.&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nThe funny thing is that Hair trigger is in the SAME expansion. &nbsp;How that issue was never considered while that expansion was being designed or during playtesting is quite surprising to me.\n</blockquote></div>\nThe same way some broken combos end up in the same set block in MtG I'd assume, just an oversight.Also remember that the original Rook City would have had far less playtesters than the more recent expansions.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Rook City was the first Expansion. There was still some learning going on. Consider that the core game was in development for a considerable period of time before it was released (Aug, 2011), and then fans immediately wanted an expansion. The Kickstarter was launched just two months later and they had proofs back from the printer by early January. Sleulth. Checmical Explosion.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>After all the in-depth discussions we've had over the years, it's sometimes hard for me to remember that there was a time early on when some of the meaning of the text and how things worked just wasn't as pinned down. I think towards the beginning there was more general acceptance that as long as you had fun playing, it didn't really matter so much how some of the arcane interactions worked. If it comes up while playing, make a decision and move on.</p>",
